{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:d9d8e8f66f0f351c1de2373f50c4bec309c3fc87623c58249c931863c0334e51","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.47},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"THIS IS WHERE WAVE 1 IS WRONG AND I AM FAR FROM THEM (36/18/39; OECD-leg marginals 0.52/0.30/0.62).\nTHE SINGLE STRONGEST SHARED ASSUMPTION, ATTACKED: all three write the core aggregate as a weighted sum of EXOGENOUS sectoral rates and conclude the level is capped because electricity is excluded from core by construction and consumer-facing AI-deflatables are only 3-8% of the basket. The identity is true and constrains nothing. Three failures. (i) DOWNWARD NOMINAL RIGIDITY: all three plug core goods at 0.3-0.5% and communication/digital at -1.0%. Those are hyper-globalization numbers (US core goods averaged ~0% 1995-2019 under maximum China supply, zero tariffs, no mineral constraint). In a tariff/fragmentation/reshoring/copper-constrained decade, sectors that \"want\" to deflate print 0 to +1.5% instead, because nominal wages and prices do not fall -- so a large relative-price skew raises the MEAN. (ii) PASS-THROUGH IN CONCENTRATED SECTORS: Panelist 2 applies a 0.45 cost-to-price pass-through to professional/financial services while simultaneously describing them as concentrated; rent concentration is a price-RAISING mechanism. The 2B panel's own modal regime (47.2%) is literally named hyperscaler rent concentration. You cannot hold both. (iii) ENDOGENOUS ANCHOR: all three assert gross debt >100% of GDP by 2029, net interest 2->3% of GDP, P95 Debt-at-Risk 117%, +1.0 pp worse (r-g), and then quietly assume the 2% target survives the window and the back half reverts. No OECD polity with a 40% old-age ratio